WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.330
Behind me is a bit of water called the Strid.

00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:04.899
There are a couple of Strids in Britain,

00:00:04.899 --> 00:00:09.460
but this is the one on the River Wharfe, at
Bolton Abbey in Yorkshire.

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:13.580
And I reckon it is the most dangerous stretch
of water anywhere on Earth.

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:16.299
Now that's a bold claim, I know.

00:00:16.299 --> 00:00:19.150
There are certainly rivers that have taken
more lives,

00:00:19.150 --> 00:00:22.250
and there are rapids and waterfalls that few
boats could ever sail.

00:00:22.250 --> 00:00:25.050
But generally, you can see them coming.

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:27.529
This is just an innocent looking stream in
the middle of some woods.

00:00:27.529 --> 00:00:29.949
You could jump over it. People occasionally
do.

00:00:29.949 --> 00:00:33.550
But if you miss that jump, it'll kill you.

00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:36.640
This is what the river looks like about a
hundred metres upstream.

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:38.460
Same river. All that water went down.

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:43.469
Thanks to the local geology, the river basically
turns on its side,

00:00:43.469 --> 00:00:46.850
gouging out passages and tunnels in the rocks
below.

00:00:46.850 --> 00:00:48.520
Those banks are actually overhangs:

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:51.079
there isn't any riverbed just below the surface,

00:00:51.079 --> 00:00:57.329
it's a deep, boiling mass of fast and deadly
currents.

00:00:57.329 --> 00:01:01.230
There are claims that falling in has a 100%
fatality rate.

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:03.140
There's no way to confirm that, of course,

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:06.980
because "local person doesn't die in river"
doesn't make the news,

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:09.500
but it has claimed a lot of lives:

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:12.900
there are even tales from the 12th century
of a young boy,

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:15.030
set to be the future king of Scotland,

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:18.290
who died trying to jump across those waters.

00:01:18.290 --> 00:01:20.200
And anything, or anyone, that falls in

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:22.880
might not come out in any recognisable form:

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:26.170
it could just get pulverised against the rocks
underwater

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:29.610
over and over and over again.

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:31.170
I'd try and put a camera in,

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:33.500
but then I'd have to get close to the edge.

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:36.990
And the edge isn't sharp, it just curves towards
the water

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:39.490
and it's covered in slippery moss.

00:01:39.490 --> 00:01:42.990
Besides, the water is opaque and brown with
peat stain,

00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:44.860
you'd see nothing.

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:49.620
Is it survivable? Maybe, with a lot of equipment
and a lot of luck.

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:52.960
And you'll find occasional testimony from
foolhardy people

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:56.090
who've swum in the calmer pools at the bottom
on drier days --

00:01:56.090 --> 00:02:00.870
but that's also where a young child drowned
back in 2010.

00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:02.210
That's why it's so dangerous.

00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:05.090
It looks safe, it looks tempting,

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:06.570
and it'll kill you.

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:09.960
